The vulnerability: `text[1024]` is a stack buffer. In the "get the server name" loop:
```c
len = (u32)strlen(test);
i = 0;
while (i<len) {
    if (test[i] == '[') is_ipv6 = GF_TRUE;
    else if (test[i] == ']') is_ipv6 = GF_FALSE;
    if ((test[i] == '/') || (!is_ipv6 && (test[i] == ':'))) break;
    text[i] = test[i];
    i += 1;
}
text[i] = 0;
```

This writes up to `len` bytes into `text[1024]` without bounds checking. If the server name portion (before `/` or `:`) is longer than 1024 bytes, it overflows the stack buffer.

Also, the first loop:
```c
i = 0;
while (i <= strlen(sURL)) {
    if (sURL[i] == ':')
        goto found;
    schema[i] = sURL[i];
    i += 1;
}
```
`schema[10]` is also a stack buffer. If the URL has no `:` within the first 10 chars, this overflows `schema`. But the URL must start with `satip://` so the `:` is at position 5, which is fine.

The key overflow is in the server name loop. The URL format is `satip://<server-name>...`. The server name is everything until `/` or `:` (when not in IPv6 brackets). So we need a URL like `satip://` followed by >1024 characters before any `/` or `:`.

